8.4  Too many open files: limits on processes

TL;DR
  - Each process inherits limits from its parent: open files, processes, stack
    size… Each has a soft value (in force) and a hard ceiling the process may
    raise it to.
  - The usual one to hit is open files: soft 1024 by default, while a busy
    server holds a descriptor per connection and per file.
  - Services started by systemd take their limits from the unit (LimitNOFILE=),
    not from your shell or limits.conf.

      ulimit -n                     # your soft limit on open files
      cat /proc/PID/limits          # a running process's limits
      ls /proc/PID/fd | wc -l       # how many files it has open now

On your machine
  Files     1,024 open per process, can be raised to 524,288
  Processes 62,844 for your user, threads included
  Stack     8.0 MiB per thread, can be raised without limit
  System    14,208 files open on the whole system

  Now       14 threads in 3 processes
  Most      supervisor, PID 4100: 12 · systemd, PID 1: 1 · rsync, PID 5000: 1
  Ceiling   126,906 threads, PIDs up to 4.2 million

Pitfalls
  - "Too many open files" can also be a leak: if the count in /proc/PID/fd keeps
    growing, a file or socket isn't being closed.
  - ulimit -n 65536 lasts for that shell and what it starts; for a service,
    systemctl edit and LimitNOFILE=65536 (22.3).
  - "fork: Resource temporarily unavailable" means a process or thread limit:
    ulimit -u, or a cgroup's pids.max (systemd's TasksMax=).
  - Containers have their own limits: docker run --ulimit nofile=….

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 8.4 --why

Related
  Commands  ulimit, prlimit, ls
  Paths     /proc/PID/limits, /etc/security/limits.conf
  See also  atfq 8.3 Event loops: one thread, thousands of connections
  See also  atfq 22.3 Unit files and overrides
  See also  atfq 16.4 cgroups
